      "updated_at": "2026-11-03T18:39:34+00:00",
      "author": "Gabriel Coler",
      "product_type": "summary",
      "bottom_line": "<p>The Central Oregon Avalanche Center is scheduled to begin avalanche forecasting on December 1.&nbsp; Hopefully we'll have enough snow to ride in the monument by then! &nbsp;For the Paulina zone we issue avalanche forecasts for every Saturday and Sunday, and usually publish a mid-week observation.&nbsp; As we start getting drips and drabs of early season snow, check out both our <a href=\"https://coavalanche.org/pages/observations#/view/observations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">observations</a> page as well as our <a href=\"https://coavalanche.org/pages/weather-map#/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">weather station map</a> for conditions.&nbsp; If we do pick up enough snow for some early season riding, remember that avalanches are possible even in relatively shallow snowpacks.&nbsp; This means that if there is enough snow to ride a sled, or make good turns, treat your travel as you would mid-winter; carry avalanche rescue gear, use thoughtful travel techniques, and assess the snow conditions.&nbsp; Places where you'll find the best early season riding like wind blown deposits are also where you are most likely to find avalanches.</p>\n<p>During November and December the avalanche center has lots of pre-season events scheduled to help you build enthusiasm for the season!&nbsp; Here is a link to our events page: &nbsp;<a href=\"https://coavalanche.org/pages/events\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://coavalanche.org/pages/events</a>.</p>",
